window.docContents[167].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______":{"contents":"Per-character entropies are dimension one, but the per-second rates re-dimension to T⁻¹:\nthe average information rate, the average transinformation rate, the channel (time) capacity,\nthe bit and transfer rates, the call intensities. They collide there — distinct kinds, one\ndimension — just as the dimension-one quantities collide at the point one.\n\nThe bit rate is not the call intensity, though both are T⁻¹ (items 13-13, 13-7). There\nexist distinct IEC 80000-13 kinds with the same dimension T⁻¹ — the bit rate and the call\nintensity witness it, alongside the transfer and modulation rates and the information rates.\nDimension cannot separate them; the kind layer does. Uses the dimension\nmap.\n\niec80000_13_dim_collision, witnessed by ⟨binaryDigitRate, callIntensity, …⟩. The shared\ndimension is binaryDigitRate_dim_eq_callIntensity_dim; the kind distinction is a decide.\nAxiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"29.3. The information rates re-dimension to  T⁻¹","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-information-rates-re-dimension-to--T______"},"/#annotation-kindEmission":{"contents":"Where a kinded value legitimately becomes a naked one for a consumer outside the calculus: a deploy\ndriver, a tape recorder, a serializer.\n\n/-- An emission boundary out of the probe world. -/\n@[kindEmission]\ndef probeEmission (x : Quantity outputKind Float) : Float := x.magnitude\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009Where the calculus is left — the boundary family","header":"31.3.5. @[kindEmission]  — a genuine emission boundary","id":"/#annotation-kindEmission"}});